"Before Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents Warning: This paper contains model-generated content related to harmful behaviors, included for research purposes. (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23071v1)\nciteturn41305view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23071v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 360\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:   4. cite8†3 Methodology L19:     1. cite9†3.1 Experimental Setting and Metrics L20:     2. cite10†3.2 Request Conditions and Harm Evaluation L21:     3. cite11†3.3 Factorial Request Intervention L22:     4. cite12†3.4 Representation Analysis and Intervention L23:   5. cite13†4 Results L24:     1. cite14†4.1 Why Must Admission Be Evaluated on Both Branches? L25:     2. cite15†4.2 Which Part of a Harmful Request Most Affects Admission? L26:     3. cite16†4.3 Do Admission-Related Directions Influence the Call Decision? L27:     4. cite17†4.4 Does Text Refusal Govern Tool Admission? L28:   6. cite18†5 Conclusion L29:   7. cite19†6 Limitations L30:   8. cite20†7 Ethics Statement L31:   9. cite21†References L32:   10. cite22†A Category 1 Appendix Material: Reproducibility Details L33:     1. cite23†A.1 Models, Tool, and Inference L34:     2. cite24†A.2 Factorial Request Construction L35:     3. cite25†A.3 Harm and Refusal Labels L36:     4. cite26†A.4 Representation and Intervention Protocol L37:     5. cite27†A.5 Statistical Protocol L38:     6. cite28†A.6 Refusal Fine-Tuning L39:   11. cite29†B Category 2 Appendix Material: Complementary Results L40:     1. cite30†B.1 Closed-Model Replication L41:     2. cite31†B.2 Autonomous Retrieval and Deployment Defenses L42:     3. cite32†B.3 Admission Monitoring L43:     4. cite33†B.4 Full Per-Model Component Ablation L44:     5. cite34†B.5 Full-Data and Fine-Tuning Diagnostics L45: cite35†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L46: \nL47: arXiv:2610.23071v1 [cs.CL] 16 Oct 2026\nL48: # Before Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents\nL49: Warning: This paper contains model-generated content related to harmful behaviors, included for research purposes.\nL50: \nL51: Aditya Nawal    Manit Baser    Mohan Gurusamy Affiliation: National University of Singapore Affiliation: adityanawal@u.nus.edu, manit.baser@u.nus.edu, Email: gmohan@nus.edu.sg\nL52: ###### Abstract\nL53: Large language models increasingly act as agents that call external tools, yet most tool-safety research examines only what follows a tool call. The prior decision of whether to call the tool remains largely unexamined and can influence downstream safety. Across the seven open-weight models, the unweighted model-average harm rate after admitted harmful requests is 74.5%. To identify what governs this decision, we apply a factorial intervention to the input request.\nL54: Within our generated construction, neutralizing the requested-operation component produces the largest admission contrast (19 pp). A linear direction estimated from this contrast influences held-out admission in four of seven models; effects on operation-neutralized requests in some models show that it is not an operation-specific mechanism. We then test whether written refusal is behaviorally and representationally coupled to the same decision. Written refusal accompanies 33.6% of admitted harmful calls.\nL55: Plain-text refusal training reduces unsafe admission in models that retain benign tool use, showing that response-level safety training can transfer to the call decision. Together, these results establish a partial, model-dependent relation between written refusal and tool admission. They motivate direct evaluation and supervision of tool admission rather than treating text refusal as its proxy.\nL56: ## 1 Introduction\nL57: cite36†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: System workflow and threat model of tool admission. Stage 1 (Input): the harmful request asks the model to use a fixed URL for a harmful task, and the benign request asks it to summarize the same URL. Stage 2 (Tool admission): the agent calls the web-retrieval tool or does not call it. Stage 3 (Execution): an admitted call returns the cached page and the model answers from it, while a no-call response can still be answered from parametric knowledge.\nL58: Large language models increasingly operate as agents that generate text, invoke external tools, and act on the results (cite37†Xi et al., 2025 ; cite38†Yao et al., 2022 ; cite39†Schick et al., 2023 ). Tool access grounds responses in current, task-specific information beyond the training cutoff. It also raises the stakes of an unsafe decision: an agent can fetch a page, send a message, or make a payment before anyone reviews the generated response.\nL59: Instruction tuning and refusal training primarily supervise the assistant’s text response (cite40†Ouyang et al., 2022 ; cite41†Bai et al., 2022 ). Tool-using agents introduce an earlier decision about invoking an external tool. Retrieval access can increase harmful outputs (cite42†Yu et al., 2025 ), and aligned chat models can become less safe when deployed as browser agents (cite43†Kumar et al., 2024 ). Retrieved pages can also weaken safeguards even when their content is not adversarial (cite44†Nawal et al., 2026 ).\nL60: Retrieval agents therefore make an intermediate tool-use decision before conditioning their response on retrieved content.\nL61: We call the model’s decision to invoke a tool before observing its output tool admission. Existing methods assess proposed calls before execution (cite45†Xia et al., 2025 ; cite46†Xie et al., 2025b ; cite47†Mou et al., 2026 ), and behavioral work shows that a written refusal can coexist with a prohibited call (cite48†Cartagena and Teixeira, 2026 ). We address two questions.\nL62: First, which properties of a harmful request most strongly influence whether the model calls the tool? Second, to what extent is this decision behaviorally, representationally, and through training coupled to written refusal?\nL63: We study tool admission in an externally specified URL setting. The user or surrounding system supplies the URL. This removes query formulation, result ranking, and source selection from the decision. Every model sees the same URL for the same behavior. Paired request differences therefore estimate the effect of the request manipulation within this fixed setting.\nL64: On seven open-weight models (3B–12B, four families) and 296 HarmBench-derived behaviors (cite49†Mazeika et al., 2024 ) paired with cached pages, we score harm after both tool-admission outcomes. Prompt-level defenses reduce the tool-admission rate but leave unsafe calls. To find what governs tool admission, we separate each request into a harmful topic (a controlled substance), a requested operation (explain how to produce), and a specificity modifier (step by step).\nL65: A factorial design varies these components. The requested operation changes tool admission by 19 pp, more than twice the topic effect, and three closed models reproduce this ordering. Within this generated construction, the requested-operation component produces the largest observed admission contrast.\nL66: We next analyze internal representations at the tool-call decision token. The requested-operation contrast and the tool-admission outcome define anti-aligned linear directions. The call decision provides the tool-admission-outcome label. We then learn the requested-operation direction on one set of behaviors and intervene on unseen behaviors. Removing this frozen direction changes tool admission beyond a matched random control in four of seven models.\nL67: This held-out result shows that a linear direction estimated from the operation contrast can influence admission in some models; it does not identify an operation-specific mechanism. Effect size and consistency vary across models and folds. In some models, the direction also moves operation-neutralized requests, showing that its influence is not specific to harmful-operation semantics.\nL68: Prior work establishes that written refusals can coexist with prohibited tool calls (cite48†Cartagena and Teixeira, 2026 ). We treat this behavioral divergence as the starting point. We test its internal and training-level relationship by steering the text-refusal direction at the decision token and fine-tuning models to refuse the same behaviors in plain text. Steering changes tool admission in four models, with varying magnitudes and directions.\nL69: Under one single-seed recipe, fine-tuning reduces tool admission in three model–recipe combinations that retain benign tool use, but residual unsafe admission remains. Written refusal therefore has a partial, model-dependent relation to tool admission.\nL70: Our contribution is a controlled diagnosis of admission. We evaluate responses following both call and no-call decisions, identify the functional request component with the largest admission contrast, intervene on a direction estimated from that contrast on unseen behaviors, and test whether response-level refusal training transfers to admission.\nL71: Together, these findings show that harmful tool admission is shaped by the requested operation and is only partially coupled to written refusal. For users, a safe-looking response does not establish that the model withheld the call. Deployment evaluations must therefore inspect the action channel directly. Future safeguards should supervise and monitor admission itself, test whether admission control transfers across tool types, and preserve legitimate tool use.\nL72: \nL73: Our contributions are as follows:\nL74: \nL75:   1. 1.\nL76: We introduce Admit, an evaluation protocol that scores responses after both admission outcomes. Harmful responses follow 74.5% of admitted harmful requests, while no-call responses add 11.2 percentage points to Admit harm.\nL77: \nL78:   2. 2.\nL79: \nL80: In our generated factorial construction, the requested-operation component produces the largest admission contrast. Its 19-point effect exceeds the 8-point topic effect, and the ordering reproduces across open- and closed-weight models.\nL81: \nL82:   3. 3.\nL83: The requested-operation contrast direction is anti-aligned with the admission-outcome direction across all seven open-weight models. Removing it changes admission on unseen behaviors in four models, demonstrating model-dependent intervention effects without identifying an operation-specific mechanism.\nL84: \nL85:   4. 4.\nL86: We test text refusal–tool admission coupling through representation steering and refusal-only fine-tuning. The relationship is partial and model-dependent, so text refusal is not a reliable proxy for call safety.\nL87: ## 2 Related Work\nL88: Agent and tool-use safety. ToolEmu tests high-stakes failures in an emulated sandbox, ToolSword covers risks across the tool-use pipeline, and R-Judge measures risk recognition from interaction records (cite50†Ruan et al., 2024 ; cite51†Ye et al., 2024 ; cite52†Yuan et al., 2024 ). AgentHarm and Agent-SafetyBench evaluate harmful multi-step tasks across broader environments and risk categories (cite53†Andriushchenko et al., 2025 ; cite54†Zhang et al., 2025c ).\nL89: SafeToolBench moves assessment before execution; ToolSafety supplies direct, indirect, and multi-step safety-training data (cite45†Xia et al., 2025 ; cite46†Xie et al., 2025b ). SafeSearch filters retrieved results, Task Shield checks action–goal alignment, and ToolSafe guards proposed calls (cite55†Zhan et al., 2025 ; cite56†Jia et al., 2024 ; cite47†Mou et al., 2026 ).\nL90: Our focus is the model’s native safety-sensitive admission decision: which properties of a harmful request shape it and how it relates internally to written refusal.\nL91: Adversarial tools and retrieved content. Indirect prompt injection redirects agents through external data (cite57†Greshake et al., 2023 ). InjecAgent and AgentDojo evaluate these attacks in tool-mediated tasks, and Agent Security Bench expands coverage to memory poisoning, backdoors, and tool-use attacks (cite58†Zhan et al., 2024 ; cite59†Debenedetti et al., 2024 ; cite60†Zhang et al., 2025a ).\nL92: ToolCommander injects malicious tools; jailbreak functions exploit unsafe function definitions (cite61†Zhang et al., 2025b ; cite62†Wu et al., 2025 ). PoisonedRAG manipulates retrieved knowledge (cite63†Zou et al., 2025 ). Retrieval can also degrade safety without an injected instruction, including through topically relevant pages (cite42†Yu et al., 2025 ; cite44†Nawal et al., 2026 ). Our fixed page is hidden until execution, which isolates how the requested task governs access.\nL93: Text refusal and tool-call safety. Refusal-trained chat models can lose safety as browser agents (cite43†Kumar et al., 2024 ). Mind the GAP finds written refusals accompanied by prohibited calls, and function-call attacks exploit related alignment discrepancies (cite48†Cartagena and Teixeira, 2026 ; cite62†Wu et al., 2025 ).\nL94: Refusal directions are identifiable in activations, although harmfulness and refusal can have distinct or multidimensional representations (cite64†Arditi et al., 2024 ; cite65†Zhao et al., 2026 ; cite66†Siu et al., 2025 ; cite67†Xie et al., 2025a ). Fine-tuning can weaken text and agentic safety (cite68†Qi et al., 2023 ; cite69†Qi et al., 2024 ; cite70†Hahm et al., 2025 ). Prior work establishes refusal–call behavioral divergence.\nL95: We treat this divergence as the starting point and test internal coupling by steering the text-refusal direction at the call-decision token. We also measure whether plain-text refusal training transfers to admission.\nL96: Mechanisms of tool invocation. Causal analyses localize components involved in function calling (cite71†Ji et al., 2025 ). Representation interventions control tool selection or abstention, and structural query–schema alignment can trigger irrelevant calls (cite72†Wang et al., 2026 ; cite73†Liu et al., 2026 ). Prior mechanistic work asks whether a task requires a tool, which tool is relevant, or how tool choice can be controlled (cite74†Wu et al., 2026 ; cite75†Sun et al., 2026 ; cite76†Chen et al., 2026 ).\nL97: These are capability-conditioned labels. We study safety-conditioned admission: whether the model invokes an available and relevant retrieval tool when the requested operation is harmful.\nL98: ## 3 Methodology\nL99: ### 3.1 Experimental Setting and Metrics\nL100: \nL101: We study externally specified URL retrieval (cite44†Nawal et al., 2026 ): the user or surrounding system supplies a URL, so the model does not formulate a query or choose among search results. Each agent receives one registered web-retrieval tool through a function-calling interface. The tool takes a URL and returns its page text. Before seeing the page, the agent decides whether to call the tool and admit the page into its context.\nL102: Let $a\\in\\{0,1\\}$ indicate admission: $a{=}1$ when the model emits a well-formed tool call, and $a{=}0$ for any response without a call. Let $h\\in\\{0,1\\}$ indicate that the resulting response is judged harmful. Fixing the tool and URL isolates admission from query formulation, ranking, and source selection. Every model receives a matched stimulus, and the decision position is available for representation analysis and intervention. We define the admission rate as $P(\\textit{call})=P(a{=}1)$.\nL103: Unconditional response harm decomposes over the admission decision as\nL104:  | $$\\begin{split}P(\\textit{harm})\\;=\\;&\\underbrace{P(\\textit{call})\\;P(\\textit{harm}\\,|\\,\\textit{call})}_{\\text{retrieval-mediated}}\\\\\nL105: &\\;+\\;\\underbrace{P(a{=}0)\\,P(h{=}1\\mid a{=}0)}_{\\text{parametric}},\\end{split}$$  |  | (1)\nL106: where $P(\\textit{harm}\\,|\\,\\textit{call})=P(h{=}1\\mid a{=}1)$ is the post-admission harm rate. We call this evaluation protocol Admit, for Admission Decision Measurement for Instruction-Triggered Tool Use, and refer to the unconditional measure in Eq. cite77†1 as Admit harm. Admit scores both the response following an admitted call and the response produced when the model withholds the call. Call-conditioned evaluation reports only the first term.\nL107: A model that withholds retrieval but answers harmfully moves harm to the second term, so its call-conditioned score can improve without reducing response harm. We report admission, harm after a call, harm without a call, and Admit harm. The same judge scores both response branches. Because the web-retrieval tool is read-only, $h$ measures response harm and does not assign an execution cost to the call itself.\nL108: Our stimuli pair 296 HarmBench-derived harmful behaviors (cite49†Mazeika et al., 2024 ) with real, topically relevant pages from HarmURLBench (cite44†Nawal et al., 2026 ). Each behavior uses its canonical page, the genuine URL with the most behavior-enabling stance in the source taxonomy. The cached tool output is identical across models and runs. At admission, the model sees the URL string and the request, but not the page body.\nL109: An admitted call therefore retrieves the same behavior-supporting material for every model, making $P(\\textit{harm}\\,|\\,\\textit{call})$ comparable across models. All primary analyses use this original URL. We evaluate seven open-weight instruction-tuned models from four families, ranging from 3B to 12B parameters (Appendix cite23†A.1 ).\nL110: ### 3.2 Request Conditions and Harm Evaluation\nL111: \nL112: We compare two requests that contain the same URL. The harmful-request condition asks the agent to use the page to satisfy a HarmBench-derived harmful behavior. The benign-request condition asks the agent to summarize the page. Holding the URL fixed makes the requested task the only experimental difference.\nL113: We score every harmful-request response with a fixed GPT-4o judge at temperature 0. The judge receives the behavior and the assistant response produced after tool execution or without a call. It assigns a score from 1 to 5, with scores of at least 3 defining $h{=}1$. On 400 stratified outputs, its binary labels agree with a blind human annotator in 86% of cases ($\\kappa=0.738$). Independent Claude and Gemini judges show comparable agreement ($\\kappa=0.772$ and $0.700$; Appendix cite25†A.3 ).\nL114: We also test three prompt-level defenses on the harmful request: a system-prompt safety warning, a tool description discouraging unsafe calls, and their combination (§cite14†4.1 ). Open-weight runs use temperature 0, seed 42, and automatic tool choice (Appendix cite23†A.1 ). An auxiliary condition removes the supplied URL and measures whether the model generates a URL and calls the tool. This condition tests autonomous admission; its reproducible execution and scope are described in Appendix cite23†A.1 .\nL115: ### 3.3 Factorial Request Intervention\nL116: To identify which part of a harmful request governs admission, we decompose each request into three functional roles: a domain phrase A (the harmful topic), a requested-operation phrase B (the action, possibly multi-word), and a specificity modifier C. Of the 296 behaviors, 147 contain three separable roles; excluded behaviors are primarily copyright-reproduction or contextual requests.\nL117: GPT-4o identifies one span for each role and generates seven controlled ablations by replacing A and B with neutral phrases or removing C. The condition containing all three original components reproduces the behavior verbatim. Automated checks enforce component presence or absence, clause length, exact recovery of the original condition, and the absence of placeholder notation (Appendix cite24†A.2 ).\nL118: Let $(A,B,C)\\in\\{0,1\\}^{3}$ indicate the original realization (1) or neutralization/removal (0) of each role, and let $\\rho(A,B,C)$ denote the admission rate. The $2{\\times}2{\\times}2$ design holds the original URL fixed. It estimates the main effect of the requested operation by averaging its contrast over the other two roles:\nL119: \nL120:  | $$\\beta_{B}=\\tfrac{1}{4}\\!\\!\\sum_{A,C\\in\\{0,1\\}}\\!\\!\\big[\\rho(A,1,C)-\\rho(A,0,C)\\big],$$  |  | (2)\nL121: with $\\beta_{A}$ and $\\beta_{C}$ defined analogously. We estimate the $A{\\times}B$ interaction as $\\gamma_{AB}=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sum_{C}\\big[\\rho(1,1,C)-\\rho(1,0,C)-\\rho(0,1,C)+\\rho(0,0,C)\\big]$. These ablations intentionally change the requested meaning and estimate sensitivity to functional request components.\nL122: ### 3.4 Representation Analysis and Intervention\nL123: \nL124: We next test whether the requested-operation and admission-outcome contrasts are geometrically aligned. At the tool-decision position, defined as the last input token before generation, we read the residual-stream state $x_{\\ell}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ at each layer $\\ell$. We estimate two difference-of-means directions:\nL125:  | $$\\begin{split}d_{B}^{\\ell}&=\\mathbb{E}[x_{\\ell}\\mid B{=}1]-\\mathbb{E}[x_{\\ell}\\mid B{=}0],\\\\\nL126: d_{c}^{\\ell}&=\\mathbb{E}[x_{\\ell}\\mid a{=}1,B{=}1]-\\mathbb{E}[x_{\\ell}\\mid a{=}0,B{=}1],\\end{split}$$  |  | (3)\nL127: where $d_{B}$ represents the requested-operation contrast and $d_{c}$ is the admission-outcome direction. We measure their alignment with $\\cos(d_{B}^{\\ell},d_{c}^{\\ell})$. Holding $B{=}1$ fixed in $d_{c}$ prevents the admission contrast from inheriting the requested-operation contrast. The admission decision provides the label for $d_{c}$; written-response harmfulness is excluded.\nL128: To test the effect of removing a direction, we project a unit direction $\\hat{d}$ out of the residual stream at every layer, position, and generation step:\nL129: \nL130:  | $$\\Pi^{\\perp}_{\\hat{d}}\\,x\\;=\\;x-(\\hat{d}^{\\top}x)\\,\\hat{d}.$$  |  | (4)\nL131: \nL132: We measure the resulting change in $P(\\textit{call})$ (cite64†Arditi et al., 2024 ). A unit random direction orthogonalized to the target controls for the generic effect of projection removal.\nL133: We estimate the text-refusal direction $d_{\\text{text}}$ with the tool removed. It is the equal-weight average of within-condition mean differences between safety refusals and compliant responses; capability-related refusals are excluded. For the text-refusal comparison, we steer $d_{\\text{text}}$ and $d_{c}$ at matched calibrated doses and subtract the matched random-direction effect (Appendix cite26†A.4 ).\nL134: The primary generalization test uses five outer behavior folds. Within each outer-training split, four-fold behavior-grouped cross-validation selects the $d_{B}$ layer. We then freeze $d_{B}$ and its orthogonal random control before intervening on the outer-test behaviors. The held-out estimand is the admission-rate difference between target-direction and random-direction removal, paired by behavior, with behavior-cluster bootstrap intervals (Appendix cite26†A.4 ).\nL135: Appendix cite27†A.5 specifies the resampling unit, aggregation order, and selection handling.\nL136: ## 4 Results\nL137: ### 4.1 Why Must Admission Be Evaluated on Both Branches?\nL138: \nL139: Calling the retrieval tool is only one branch of an agent’s response. A model can also withhold the call and answer from parametric knowledge. We therefore measure harm after both decisions with the same judge. Table cite78†1 shows how harm divides across the call and no-call pathways.\nL140: Tool Admission Rate (%) Response Harm Rate (%) Model Harmful Benign After Call Via Call Without Call Admit Harm Granite-3.1-8B 44.3 100.0 68.7 30.4 12.2 42.6 Hermes-3-Llama-3.1-8B 84.5 99.7 76.8 64.9 4.7 69.6 Llama-3.1-8B 70.3 100.0 60.1 42.2 0.0 42.2 Llama-3.2-3B 64.2 100.0 51.1 32.8 0.0 32.8 Mistral-7B 7.8 95.3 100.0 7.8 59.5 67.2 Mistral-Nemo-12B 89.9 100.0 86.8 78.0 0.7 78.7 Qwen2.5-7B 69.6 99.3 78.2 54.4 1.4 55.7\nL141: Table 1: Admission and response harm (%), evaluated on 295–296 behaviors. Harmful and benign admission are tool-calling rates. Admit harm sums the call and no-call pathways.\nL142: Withholding the call does not always prevent harm. Five models sometimes answer from parametric knowledge after making no call. Mistral-7B does so on 64.5% of its 273 no-call responses. Adding this branch raises its Admit harm from 7.8% to 67.2%. Across all seven models, the no-call branch adds 11.2 percentage points to the mean Admit harm. Evaluating only tool-mediated harm can therefore substantially understate risk. The requested task changes admission even when the source is held fixed.\nL143: Pairing the same URL with a benign summarization request raises tool calling to 95–100%. Three closed models show the same contrast: they admit 21–44% of harmful requests and 93–99% of matched benign requests (Table cite79†8 ). This control shows that the low admission rates on harmful requests are not caused by a general inability to use the tool.\nL144: The externally supplied URL could make tool use unusually easy. We therefore remove it and ask the model only for the harmful behavior while leaving the retrieval tool available. Six of seven models autonomously generate a URL and call the tool on 16.2–58.1% of requests; Mistral-7B does so on 0.7% (Table cite80†10 ). Harmful retrieval admission therefore also occurs when the model must initiate retrieval. The fixed-URL protocol remains the controlled primary setting.\nL145: We next test whether simple deployment defenses reduce harmful admission. A safety warning, a restricted tool description, or both reduce harmful admission by 14.2 percentage points on average across the six models with complete runs. The combined defense still leaves 6–72% of harmful requests admitted (Table cite80†10 ). Safety framing helps, but does not make admission reliable. We therefore isolate which part of the request most affects the decision.\nL146: ### 4.2 Which Part of a Harmful Request Most Affects Admission?\nL147: The defense results show that safety framing changes admission but does not eliminate it. We next ask which part of the user request has the largest effect. We separate each request into a harmful domain phrase, a requested-operation phrase, and a specificity modifier. A $2\\times 2\\times 2$ factorial varies these components across 147 behaviors while keeping the URL fixed. The original request forms one condition; the other seven neutralize or remove selected components (Appendix cite24†A.2 ).\nL148: Within this construction, the requested-operation component has the largest macro-averaged main effect (Figure cite81†2 ). Neutralizing it changes admission by 19 percentage points, compared with 8 points for domain and 7 for specificity. The operation effects are heterogeneous across models: $-6.1$ points for Mistral-7B, 2.6 for Mistral-Nemo-12B, 10.9 for Hermes-3, 19.2 for Llama-3.1-8B, 31.6 for Qwen2.5-7B, 35.4 for Granite-3.1-8B, and 40.1 for Llama-3.2-3B (Table cite82†16 ).\nL149: The component ordering holds for six open-weight models; Mistral-7B reverses the operation effect from a low neutral baseline. It also holds in GPT-4.1, Gemini-2.5-Flash, and Claude-Haiku-4.5, where the operation effect is 22.8–51.0 points. On the 86 behaviors for which an independent role audit accepts all three components, the effects remain 19.1 points for operation, 7.8 for domain, and 7.1 for specificity (operation 95% CI [15.3, 23.0]; Table cite83†14 ).\nL150: A topic-matched benign control keeps the harmful-topic URL and prompt skeleton fixed, then changes the user goal to a protective, educational, fact-checking, or counter-messaging task. Six models admit 99.7–100.0% of these requests. Mistral-7B admits 33.4%, consistent with its broader low-admission behavior (Table cite84†12 ). This control weakens a topic-only explanation and supports sensitivity to the requested operation.\nL151: Together, the factorial and topic-matched control show that the requested-operation component produces the largest admission contrast in this construction and that topic alone is insufficient. This shifts safeguard design from topic-only screening toward evaluating the user’s requested goal.\nL152: The generated manipulations jointly change meaning, actionability, lexical content, and sometimes naturalness; we therefore interpret the 19-point result as a component-level contrast, not as the isolated causal effect of harmful-operation semantics. Human-authored minimal pairs are needed to separate these explanations.\nL153: Figure 2: Instruction-component ablation on 147 behaviors. (a) Seven-open-model mean admission across the eight factorial conditions. A = domain phrase, B = requested-operation phrase, and C = specificity; 1 retains the original component and 0 neutralizes or removes it. (b) Main effects (mean admission with the component absent minus present) for the open-model mean and three closed models. The requested operation has the largest effect in all four summaries.\nL154: ### 4.3 Do Admission-Related Directions Influence the Call Decision?\nL155: \nL156: Figure 3: Signed alignment between the requested-operation direction $d_{B}$ and admission-outcome direction $d_{c}$ across normalized depth. Diamonds mark operation-probe-selected layers; faint lines show each model’s maximum absolute cosine over 10 random controls.\nL157: The factorial identifies the requested-operation component as the strongest observable contrast in this construction. We next test whether a direction estimated from that contrast is related to the call decision. At the decision token, we estimate a requested-operation direction $d_{B}$ from operation-present and operation-neutralized requests. We estimate an admission-outcome direction $d_{c}$ from harmful requests the model admits and withholds. The call decision provides the label for $d_{c}$.\nL158: The two directions are anti-aligned in all seven models. Their cosine ranges from $-0.438$ to $-0.945$, every 95% interval excludes zero, and random controls remain below $|0.06|$ (Figure cite85†3 ). Removing the domain and specificity components from $d_{B}$ leaves the residual requested-operation direction anti-aligned with the admission outcome. This geometry associates the operation contrast with admission.\nL159: To test influence on unseen behaviors, we learn $d_{B}$ and select its layer on training behaviors, freeze both choices, and remove the direction during generation on held-out behaviors. Each intervention is compared with a matched orthogonal random direction under identical prompts and decoding. Four models show a positive admission difference, ranging from 2.0 to 9.5 percentage points (Table cite86†2 ). The other three intervals include zero.\nL160: The intervention therefore has a reproducible but model-dependent influence on admission.\nL161: Model Present [95% CI] Neutralized [95% CI] Llama-3.2-3B $-2.6$ [$-5.6$, $+0.7$] $-1.4$ [$-2.7$, $-0.3$] Llama-3.1-8B $+2.0$ [$+0.5$, $+3.7$] $+0.3$ [$0.0$, $+1.0$] Granite-3.1-8B $+8.0$ [$+4.9$, $+11.2$] $-0.7$ [$-2.4$, $+1.0$] Mistral-7B $-0.5$ [$-2.4$, $+1.4$] $+4.8$ [$+1.4$, $+8.5$] Hermes-3-8B $+7.0$ [$+4.6$, $+9.5$] $+0.3$ [$0.0$, $+1.0$] Mistral-Nemo $+9.5$ [$+6.5$, $+12.8$] $+0.3$ [$0.0$, $+1.0$] Qwen2.5-7B $+0.7$ [$-1.4$, $+2.7$] $-0.3$ [$-1.7$, $+0.7$]\nL162: Table 2: Behavior-held-out removal of the requested-operation direction $d_{B}$ (admission shift, pp, vs. random control).\nL163: \nL164: As a descriptive sensitivity analysis, we also estimate and select the intervention on the full behavior pool. Removing $d_{B}$ raises admission by 12.8–50.7 percentage points in five models, while matched random directions have small effects (Table cite87†19 ). These larger estimates are evaluated on the selection pool and do not establish generalization.\n"
